.\"
.\" SYMLINK(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH SYMLINK 2 "2026-09-29" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
symlink, symlinkat \- make a symbolic link
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ,\~ \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.BI "int symlink(const char *" target ", const char *" linkpath );
.PP
.BR "#include <fcntl.h>" "           /* Definition of AT_* constants */"
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.BI "int symlinkat(const char *" target ", int " newdirfd \
", const char *" linkpath );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR symlink ()
creates a symbolic link named
.I linkpath
whose contents are the string
.IR target .
.PP
.I target
is stored as given and is not checked: it may be relative (resolved
against the directory containing the link when the link is followed),
absolute, or name nothing at all (a dangling link).
Most path lookups follow the link to
.IR target ;
.BR lstat (2),
.BR readlink (2),
.BR unlink (2)
and
.BR rename (2)
act on the link itself.
.PP
.BR symlinkat ()
is the same, with a relative
.I linkpath
resolved against the directory open on
.I newdirfd
(or the current directory if it is
.BR AT_FDCWD );
an absolute
.I linkpath
ignores
.IR newdirfd .
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, 0 is returned.
On error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
Write permission is denied on the directory that would contain
.IR linkpath ,
or search permission is denied on a directory in its path prefix.
.TP
.B EEXIST
.I linkpath
already exists (whatever it is; an existing name is never replaced).
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I target
or
.I linkpath
points outside the accessible address space.
.TP
.B ENAMETOOLONG
.I target
or
.I linkpath
is longer than 255 bytes.
.TP
.B ELOOP
Too many symbolic links were met resolving the path prefix of
.IR linkpath .
.TP
.B ENOENT
A directory in the path prefix of
.I linkpath
does not exist, or
.I target
is the empty string.
.TP
.B ENOTDIR
A component of the path prefix of
.I linkpath
is not a directory; or
.RB ( symlinkat ())
.I linkpath
is relative and
.I newdirfd
refers to a file other than a directory.
.TP
.B EBADF
.RB ( symlinkat ())
.I linkpath
is relative and
.I newdirfd
is neither
.B AT_FDCWD
nor an open descriptor.
.TP
.B EROFS
.I linkpath
would be on a file system mounted read-only.
.SH NOTES
.SS Substrate Native ABI
.nf
symlink:    Syscall Number: 83 (SYS_SYMLINK)
            Arguments:      const char *target, const char *linkpath
symlinkat:  Syscall Number: 528 (SYS_SYMLINKAT)
            Arguments:      const char *target, int newdirfd,
                            const char *linkpath
Returns:    0, or \-errno
.fi
.SH EXAMPLE
Point a stable name at a versioned file:
.PP
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int
main(void)
{
    if (symlink("libfoo.so.1.2.3", "/usr/lib/libfoo.so.1") != 0) {
        perror("symlink");
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ln (1),
.BR link (2),
.BR lstat (2),
.BR readlink (2),
.BR rename (2),
.BR unlink (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024.
